{"concepts_used":["GC-0046","GC-0180"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMA1995/s202","quote":"The Registrar may, for the purposes of this Act:"},{"ref":"TMA1995/s202","quote":"(c) require the production of documents or articles;"},{"ref":"TMM/Part61/3/3/1","quote":"a document that would be privileged from production in legal proceedings on the ground of legal professional privilege"},{"ref":"TMM/Part61/3/3/1","quote":"a document that is subject to an order of a court or tribunal that prohibits disclosure of the document or information in the document"},{"ref":"TMM/Part61/3/3/1","quote":"a document: whose production the Registrar has required under paragraph 202(c) of the Act; and that the Registrar is satisfied should not be made available for public inspection"}],"answer":"**Yes—the Registrar has a statutory power to require documents.** Section 202(c) of the Trade Marks Act 1995 expressly permits the Registrar to “require the production of documents or articles”. The opening words limit that power to “the purposes of this Act”. [TMA1995/s202]

**When does that go too far?** The express boundary established by the supplied legislation is that statutory purpose: section 202 does not give an unrestricted power to demand documents for any purpose. However, these passages do not explain the tests for relevance, excessive breadth, oppressive demands or how to challenge a production requirement. They therefore do not establish precisely when a particular demand would exceed the power. [TMA1995/s202]

**Production and public disclosure are distinct issues in the Manual’s practice.** The Manual identifies documents whose production has been required under section 202(c) but which the Registrar is satisfied should not be available for public inspection. It also lists documents attracting legal professional privilege and documents subject to court or tribunal orders prohibiting disclosure. These are Manual statements, not additional statutory rules. The supplied excerpt does not establish whether, or how, privilege or a disclosure prohibition permits someone to resist production to the Registrar; it should not be treated as answering that question. [TMM/Part61/3/3/1]"}
